    "case_summary": "A man in his 40s with unilateral otomandibular syndrome (OMS) associated with severe temporal bone hypoplasia had undergone many mandibular and auricular reconstruction surgeries in childhood. He presented with a history of deep unilateral neck cellulitis. The first episode occurred in 2014 with no obvious cause. The second episode occurred in 2021, with discharge through the artificial ear canal. Two months after the second episode, the patient experienced another recurrence of neck cellulitis.\n\nDischarge was noted through the artificial ear canal during the second episode of cellulitis.\n\n- Imaging Studies:    1. Initial CT scan:       - Title: Axial Plane CT Scan       - Description: Showed collection in the right parapharyngeal space and neck collection. A hypodense lesion was noted in the middle ear.        2. Follow-up MRI and CT scan:       - Title: Contrast-Enhanced Coronal Plane MRI       - Description: Revealed a fistula originating from the middle ear       - Title: Axial plane diffusion-weighted MRI       - Description: Showed a hyperintense 23-mm lesion at the edge of the petrous bone        3. Surgical Findings:       - 2-centimeter mass found during drilling of the hypoplastic petrous bone       - Millimetric fistula at the tip of the mastoid running into the sternocleidomastoid muscle",
